# The guild factor: the Wayfarers' man in every friendly town (Adventure
# Mode; spawned by MapComponent_TSC_GuildFactor on settlement maps). The
# hall made flesh: the contract board, hirelings, and the rates haggle.
# He is generated, not a named NPC - the dialogue carries the character.

dialogue TSC_Dialogue_GuildFactor

== start ==
The guild's factor works from a table near the middle of town: ledger, ink, a strongbox chained to the table leg, and the Wayfarers' mark on a board behind him. He reads your charter upside down without asking for it.

"A charter rider. Good. There is more work on the roads than the guild can write down. What do you need?"

* "What work is posted?" -> board
    do contract_board()
* "I'm looking to hire on help." -> hire
    do hire_offer()
* "I've coin of the charter to spend." -> locker
    do guild_store()
* "The guild's rates are thin for what the roads cost. Sweeten them." -> rates_up
    if not flag(TSC_GuildRates)
    check Persuasion 8 -> rates_up | rates_flat
* [Set the toll book on his ledger, open at the countersigned page.] -> ledger
    if flag(TSC_LedgerRead) and quest_active(TSC_Quest_LedgerChoice)
* [Hand over the sealed dispatch from the dead courier.] -> dispatch
    if has_item(TSC_SealedDispatch)
    do take_item(TSC_SealedDispatch)
    do give_item(Silver, 120)
    do grant_xp(40)
* "Nothing today." -> end

== dispatch ==
He reads the direction, then the seal, and his face does the small arithmetic of a man recognizing a dead colleague's handwriting.

"That rider was due in the spring." He sets the packet unopened in the strongbox and counts silver onto the table without being asked. "The guild pays for its mail, delivered by whoever delivers it. That's the whole of the custom, and it's a good one. Where you found this - I won't ask. The roads keep what they keep."

* "The roads keep what they keep." -> start
* "That's all our business today." -> end

# The contract board window opens the moment "What work is posted?" is
# chosen (the effect rides that option), so by the time the player reads
# THIS node they have already looked - the window was just closed over it.
# The node is written for that moment: continuations, not an invitation
# to do what was just done.
== board ==
He lets you read as long as you care to, working his ledger the while.

"Take what suits. Pay is on completion, as chartered."

* "Show me the board again." -> board
    do contract_board()
* "Other business, then." -> start
* "That's the lot for today." -> end

== ledger ==
He reads it upside down out of habit, then turns it round and reads it properly.

The pen goes down. He does not reach for the book, and he does not look at the door.

"That is a guild hand," he says. "You will have worked out whose."

He is quiet for a moment. When he speaks again the professional manner is gone.

"The charter says I should put you in irons for this. It also says the man who signed those margins draws the same wage I do." He folds his hands. "It is your book. Tell me what you want to happen and I will tell you what it costs."

* "The guild should have it. Let them deal with their own." -> ledger_in
    do flag(TSC_LedgerTurnedIn); signal(TSC_Quest_LedgerChoice, TSC_LedgerTurnIn); grant_xp(25)
* "It is worth more to you than it is to them. Name a price." -> ledger_out
    do flag(TSC_FactorBought); signal(TSC_Quest_LedgerChoice, TSC_LedgerSell); grant_xp(25)
* "I have not decided yet." -> start

== ledger_in ==
He takes the book carefully and puts it in the chained strongbox rather than a drawer.

"It goes up the road tonight, and I will not be told what happens after that." He picks the pen back up. "For what it is worth: there are riders who would have sold me that, and I would have paid."

He looks at you properly for the first time since you came in.

"The guild will hear your company's name, and hear it well. That counts for something out here."

* "See that it does." -> start

== ledger_out ==
He does not haggle, which tells you that you asked for too little. He counts it out of the strongbox in front of you.

The book goes into the brazier. He watches until the signed page has burned through.

"A pleasure," he says, and means it, though something in his face has closed. "The standard stock is always available to you. Charter goods, posted rates. Everything a rider needs."

He does not mention the back of the case. It stays under the table.

* "A pleasure." -> start

== locker ==
He reaches past the chained strongbox and brings up a flat iron case that opens on both sides.

"Charter goods. The guild pays in coin because coin is the only thing this case takes. Silver buys what any road buys. These buy what the guild wants its people carrying."

* "Let me see the case." -> start
    do guild_store()
* "Another time." -> start

== hire ==
"There is always somebody in a town like this wanting out of it." He runs a finger down the ledger without looking. "The guild checks them enough to know which end of the work they hold. The rest you find out on the road."

* "Understood." -> start

== rates_up ==
The factor puts his pen down.

"The roads cost what they cost," he says. "And the guild knows what its riders have been through this season. All right. Charter rate plus a fifth, marked to your name. Do not tell the next rider."

* "Done. Show me the board." -> board
    do flag(TSC_GuildRates); grant_xp(25); contract_board()
* "Done, and appreciated." -> start
    do flag(TSC_GuildRates); grant_xp(25)

== rates_flat ==
"The rates are the rates." He keeps hold of the pen. "The guild pays on completion at charter terms, the same as every rider in this ledger. The board is behind me if that suits you."

* "Fair enough. Show me the board." -> board
    do contract_board()
* "Fair enough." -> start
